  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 AM Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nA very large and strong monsoon ridge jumps out at you as soon\nas you open satellite imagery this morning. %00mb heights are\n>596 dm over TX/NM and over 590 dm over the quad state with\nnorthwest flow. \"Ridge runner\" convection is spiraling around\nthis system and timing out the activity/wave over KS/NE puts it\ninto southeast Missouri by late this afternoon. This should give\nus at least a chance for another round of thunderstorms. Surface\ndewpoints remain exceptionally high with widespread 80+\ndewpoints this morning. With skies largely clearing behind the\nlast wave of convection another day of excessive heat appears\nlikely and kept the warning/advisory largely as is save adding\ntwo Kentucky Pennyrile counties.\n\nA shortwave trough over the Great Lakes region looks likely to\npush a modest front through tonight. This new airmass should see\ndewpoints Wednesday falling into the mid 60s with highs in the\nupper 80s, which is plenty hot but much closer to seasonal\nnorms.\n\nThrough the week the monsoon ridge shifts westward and a\nshortwave trough flops in over the top and into the area.\nGFS/ECMWF are fairly consistent with the way this system \nevolves bringing some rain and thunderstorm chances Saturday \nand Sunday but the run-to-run continuity is poor and this \nevolution looks like it could evolve more from a fast moving \nshortwave into more of a lumbering closed off low and I would \nnot be at all surprised to see fairly significant shifts in the\noverall forecast picture as we approach the weekend. &&",
